@startuml leader-election
title US-07: Leader Election - One Instance Wins the Lease

skinparam sequenceArrowThickness 1.5
skinparam participantBorderColor #555555
skinparam participantBackgroundColor #F8F8F8
skinparam noteBorderColor #AAAAAA
skinparam noteBackgroundColor #FFFDE7

participant "Evaluator\nInstance A" as A
participant "Evaluator\nInstance B" as B
database "Redis\nalert-evaluator:leader" as Redis
queue "Kafka\nalerts" as Kafka

note over A, B
  Both instances start simultaneously.
  Both attempt to acquire the leader lease.
end note

A -> Redis : SET alert-evaluator:leader $instance_a NX PX $TTL_MS
B -> Redis : SET alert-evaluator:leader $instance_b NX PX $TTL_MS

note over Redis
  NX = only set if key does not exist.
  PX = expire after TTL_MS milliseconds.
  Atomic. Exactly one instance wins.
end note

Redis --> A : OK (lease acquired - Instance A is leader)
Redis --> B : nil (lease already held - Instance B is follower)

note over B
  Follower polls the lease key.
  Runs no evaluation.
  Emits no alerts.
  Waits for the key to expire.
end note

loop every heartbeat interval (< TTL_MS)
  A -> Redis : Lua: if GET('alert-evaluator:leader') == $instance_a\nthen PEXPIRE('alert-evaluator:leader', $TTL_MS)
  note over Redis
    GET-then-PEXPIRE via Lua (atomic).
    SET XX PX is NOT safe for renewal —
    it overwrites the key value without checking
    who holds it; a slow old leader could
    accidentally steal a lease held by a new leader.
    Lua compare-and-expire prevents this.
  end note
  A -> Kafka : emit alerts (leader only)
end

@enduml
